#* {{quote-book|en|year=2001|author=w:Richard Flanagan|title={{w|Gould's Book of Fish|Gould’s Book of Fish}}|location=New York|publisher=Grove Press|year_published=2002|chapter=The Freshwater Crayfish|url=https://books.google.ca/books?id=VRPnBAAAQBAJ&printsec=frontcover#v=onepage&q&f=false
|passage={{...}} she brought a red daubed finger up to my cheek & began to '''smear''' markings on my face.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause (something) to be a particular colour by covering with a substance.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1864|author={{w|Richard Francis Burton|Richard F. Burton}}|title=A Mission to Gelele, King of Dahome,|location=London|publisher=Tinsley Brothers|volume=1|chapter=3|page=43|url=https://archive.org/details/missiontogelelek18641burt/page/43/mode/1up?q=smeared
|passage=small wooden dolls '''smeared''' red as though with blood}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1917|author=w:William Carlos Williams|chapter=Pastoral|title=w:Al Que Quiere!|location=Boston|publisher=The Four Seas Company|page=15|url=https://archive.org/details/bookofpoem100willrich/page/15/mode/1up?q=smeared
|passage=the fences and outhouses<br>built of barrel-staves<br>and parts of boxes, all,<br>if I am fortunate,<br>'''smeared''' a bluish green}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1993|author=w:Vikram Seth|title=w:A Suitable Boy|publisher=Penguin|year_published=1994|section=Chapter 2.1, p. 73|url=https://archive.org/details/suitableboy0000seth/page/73/mode/1up?q=smeared
|passage=They paid the [[tonga#Etymology_1|tonga]]-[[wallah#Etymology_1|wallah]] double his regular fare and '''smeared''' his forehead pink and that of his horse green for good measure.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To rub (a body part, etc.) across a surface.
#* {{RQ:Dickens Great Expectations|I|III|37|text={{...}} he '''smeared''' his ragged rough sleeve over his eyes.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1979|author=w:William Styron|title={{w|Sophie's Choice|Sophie’s Choice}}|url=https://archive.org/details/sophieschoicesty00styr/page/58/mode/1up?q=smeared|chapter=3|page=58|publisher=Random House|location=New York
|passage=With the lazy appetite of a calf mooning over a salt lick, he '''smeared''' his sizable nose against her face,}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2013|author=w:Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie|title=w:Americanah|url=https://archive.org/details/americanah00chim/page/74/mode/1up?q=%22smear+their%22|chapter=6|page=74|publisher=Knopf|location=New York
|passage={{...}} what was it with all those village people who could not stand on their feet without reaching out to '''smear''' their palm on a wall?}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To attempt to remove (a substance) from a surface by rubbing.
#* {{RQ:Dickens Oliver Twist|1|13|page=198|text=He had {{...}} a dirty [[belcher#Etymology_2|belcher]] handkerchief round his neck, with the long frayed ends of which he '''smeared''' the beer from his face as he spoke:}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1926|author=w:D. H. Lawrence|title=w:The Plumed Serpent|location=London|publisher=Heinemann|year_published=1955|chapter=5|page=85|url=https://archive.org/details/plumedserpent0000lawr/page/76/mode/2up?q=smear
|passage=The boatman rowed short and hard {{...}}, only pausing at moments swiftly to '''smear''' the sweat from his face with an old rag he kept on the bench beside him.}}
#* '''1960''', {{w|Katherine Anne Porter}}, “Holiday” in Douglas and Sylvia Angus (eds.), ''Contemporary American Short Stories,'' New York: Ballantine, 1983, p.{{nbsp}}323,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/contemporaryamer00angu/page/323/mode/1up?q=smearing]</sup>
#*: {{...}} she stood and shook with silent crying, '''smearing''' away her tears with the open palm of her hand.
# {{lb|en|climbing}} To climb without using [[foothold]]s, using the friction from the shoe to stay on the wall.

