===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}
[[File:Bacterial smear on nutrient agar - E. coli.JPG|thumb|A bacterial smear]]

# A mark made by smearing.
#: {{syn|en|streak}}
#: ''This detergent cleans windows without leaving '''smears'''.''
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1886|author=w:Thomas Hardy|title=w:The Mayor of Casterbridge|location=London|publisher=Smith, Elder|volume=2|chapter=8|page=108|url=https://archive.org/details/mayorofcasterbri02hard/page/108/mode/1up?q=smear
|passage=A '''smear''' of decisive lead-coloured paint had been laid on to obliterate Henchard’s name, though its letters dimly loomed through like ships in a fog.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1933|author=w:Robert Byron|title=First Russia, Then Tibet|location=London|publisher=Macmillan|section=Part 2, Chapter 8|url=https://www.fadedpage.com/books/20170539/html.php
|passage=Vast avalanches had left their dirty '''smears''' on the opposing slopes,}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1952|author=w:Nevil Shute|title={{w|The Far Country (novel)|The Far Country}}|location=London|publisher=Heinemann|chapter=2|url=https://www.fadedpage.com/books/20120508/html.php
|passage=she bought a couple of rolls filled with a thin '''smear''' of potted meat for her breakfast}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2005|author=w:John Banville|title={{w|The Sea (novel)|The Sea}}|location=London|publisher=Picador|section=Part 2, p. 228|url=https://archive.org/details/sea00banv/page/228/mode/1up?q=smear
|passage=I could see the roofs of the town on the horizon, and farther off and higher up, a tiny silver ship propped motionless on a '''smear''' of pale sea.}}
# {{lb|en|countable|uncountable}} A [[false]] or [[unsupported]], [[malicious]] statement intended to [[injure]] a person's [[reputation]].
#: {{syn|en|calumny|slander|slur|mudslinging}}
#: {{ux|en|[[smear campaign|'''smear''' campaign]]}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1752|author=w:Theophilus Cibber|title=A Lick at a Liar|location=London|publisher=R. Griffiths|page=7|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004806911.0001.000
|passage=I should have held him quite beneath my Notice, as is all he utters, but that the Appetite of Slander, in many, is too predominant; and, ’tis possible, when the filthiest Fellow throws a Profusion of Dirt, some may chance to stick, if not timely thrown off; I shall endeavour therefore, to wipe away the sooty '''Smears''' of this Chimney-sweeper, by relating a simple Fact, which will, I flatter myself, amply confute the malicious Tales of this unprovoked, rancorous Mortal:}}
#* {{RQ:Toole Confederacy Dunces|page=289|chapter=13|passage=“I’d rather not [read the newspaper article]. It’s probably full of falsification and '''smear'''. The yellow journalists doubtlessly suggested all sorts of lip-smacking innuendoes.”}}
# {{lb|en|biology}} A [[preparation]] to be [[examine]]d under a [[microscope]], made by spreading a thin layer of a substance (such as blood, bacterial culture) on a [[slide#Noun|slide]].<ref>Edwin Benzel Steen, ''Dictionary of Biology,'' New York: Barnes & Noble, 1971.<sup>[https://archive.org/details/dictionaryofbiol00edwi/page/504/mode/1up?q=smear]</sup></ref>
#: {{syn|en|squash}}
# {{lb|en|medicine}} A [[Pap smear]] {{gloss|screening test for cervical cancer}}.
#: {{syn|en|cervical smear|Pap test}}
#: ''I'm going to the doctor's this afternoon for a '''smear'''.''
# {{lb|en|radio|television|uncountable}} Any of various forms of [[distortion]] that make a [[signal]] harder to see or hear.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1954|title=Radio & Television News: Radio-electronic engineering section
|passage=In television terms, a certain amount of '''smear''', ringing, and anticipatory overshoot are indigenous to VSB transmission.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1972|title=Scientific and Technical Aerospace Reports
|passage=Results show the reduction in intelligibility produced by changing the filter condition was much greater than reductions caused by altering '''smear''' duration.}}
# {{lb|en|climbing}} A [[maneuver]] in which the shoe is placed onto the [[hold]]less rock, and the friction from the shoe keeps it in contact
# {{lb|en|music}} A rough [[glissando]] in [[jazz]] music.

